                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "<B>Summary</B><BR><BR>\nThis qualitative study explored the challenges dental students face in transferring knowledge from the classroom to clinical practice, specifically in the context of primary teeth pulpotomy. Sixteen semi-structured interviews were conducted with both expert and novice participants to investigate factors that complicate clinical management and clinical reasoning. Thematic analysis revealed three overarching themes—child-related, parent-related, and student-related factors—each contributing to difficulties in clinical practice. Within each theme, sub-themes highlighted common errors and complicating factors. The findings offer valuable insights for developing targeted simulation-based educational programs aimed at bridging the gap between theoretical knowledge and clinical application in dental education.<BR><BR>\n<B>Dataset</B><BR><BR>\nThe dataset includes anonymized qualitative data from 16 semi-structured interviews with dental education experts and novice students. It consists of, ‘coded excerpts’, contains selected excerpts from interview transcripts along with thematic codes assigned during the analysis; and, ‘codebook’, details the thematic structure, including main themes and their respective sub-themes. This dataset was used to identify critical factors affecting clinical management and reasoning, guiding the design of educational simulations in primary teeth pulpotomy.<BR><BR>"
                },
                "dsDescriptionDate": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionDate",
                  "multiple": false,
